/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_BuildGuide_MacOs
 *
 * Supported target presets:
 *
 * @code
 * macos_x64
 * macos_arm64
 * @endcode
 *
 * Both presets use Apple Clang and Ninja Multi-Config. `macos_arm64` sets
 * `CMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES=arm64`; `macos_x64` sets it to `x86_64`. The compiler
 * version and deployment environment are still part of practical compatibility
 * and should be recorded with release artifacts.
 *
 * Each preset uses one multi-configuration build tree. Select Debug or Release
 * at build time; both archives use the same artifact and distribution directory.
 *
 * Apple Silicon and Intel builds must be treated as different artifacts unless
 * a universal binary strategy is explicitly implemented.
 * The current workflow creates thin, architecture-specific archives rather than
 * combining them with `lipo`.
 *
 * Day-to-day incremental build:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug --target Foundation
 * @endcode
 *
 * The build script configures the preset automatically. A complete manual
 * release-candidate sequence is documented in
 * @ref Foundation_Workflows_Manual.
 *
 * Clean release export:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * xcode-select -p
 * ninja --version
 * ./scripts/configure.sh macos_arm64 --fresh
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Release --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh
 * @endcode
 *
 * The export contains `libFoundation_d.a` and `libFoundation.a`.
 * Examples are excluded by default. Add `--examples-on` when the package should
 * also contain Release API example executables. The package then includes one
 * `FoundationExamples.command` launcher that can be opened from Finder. It shows
 * a menu, runs the selected console binary, reports its status, and returns to
 * the menu. Window closure after choosing `q` follows
 * **Terminal > Settings > Profiles > Shell >
 * When the shell exits**. **Close if the shell exited cleanly** closes successful
 * examples while leaving a failed example visible for diagnosis.
 * Verify both with:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * lipo -info dist/macos_arm64/lib/libFoundation_d.a
 * lipo -info dist/macos_arm64/lib/libFoundation.a
 * @endcode
 *
 * Repeat with `macos_x64`; both commands must then report `x86_64`. An Intel
 * consumer may require Rosetta to execute on Apple Silicon, but archive creation
 * and architecture inspection do not.
 *
 * Native CTest execution is possible, but no tests are currently registered.
 * See @ref Foundation_Workflows_Test and use the acceptance sequence in
 * @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation.
 */
